en:
Oh, everyone, I should have mentioned this sooner, but I have a very strong sixth sense.
Especially when it comes to restrooms.
I find it odd myself, but whenever I get near a restroom, I pick up a strange aura.{p}

That's right.
He decided to burn that restroom down.
He brought gasoline and snuck into the school in the middle of the night.{p}

"Aaaaaaaah!"
He screamed at the top of his lungs and flung the gasoline in his hand all over the restroom.
The gasoline flew through the air and the bugs swarmed onto him at almost the same moment.{p}

"Aaaaaaaaagh!"
Only his dying scream echoed through the silent darkness.
The only ones who heard his last scream were the bugs, hundreds, thousands of them, in countless numbers...{p}

And his wide-open mouth was crammed full of dead bugs.
When they did an autopsy later, they found bugs packed tight all the way down into his stomach.
And apparently bugs had crawled into his nostrils and his ears too.{p}

Gross story, huh?
That's why the spirits of bugs swirl around in that restroom.
The grudge of the countless bugs killed by a cruel boy has soaked into that restroom.{p}

But now it's different.
I believe it.
Why did I come to believe it, you ask?{p}

What he was eating was flies.
His lunch box was heaped full of flies.
He knew I was right beside him, but he kept eating, completely absorbed.{p}

When I got back to the classroom, class was still going on.
The teacher was startled, because my face was white as a sheet.
And then I collapsed right there.{p}

I couldn't make a sound, so I just lay there, still.
Tsuda had that look in his eyes.
He was looking at me the way you look at food.{p}

After he'd kept that up for a while, he felt, though maybe it was his imagination, that there were more and more bugs.
"Why★"
he muttered.{p}

Then he noticed that a single moth as big as a grown-up's face had come flying in, maybe drawn by the restroom light.
The moth fluttered around him, and it looked as if it were mocking him.
"Are you the boss☆"{p}

And then... it felt different from usual.
There was no hard floor; the footing felt somehow unsteady and squishy.
When he pressed down and put his weight on his foot, it felt like stepping on a thick, fluffy carpet.{p}

Well, maybe a story like that could exist.
The world's a big place, after all...
And you know, that "maybe" actually came true.{p}

And then Tsuda suddenly turned serious and said this.
"...You know, I can't remember the last few days.
I wonder what happened to me.{p}

Is that so? That's fine.
You don't have to believe me...
Then would you try going to that restroom alone for me?{p}

Huh, you like fortune-telling? You're {g}{f:just like a girl|a girl, all right}.
I believe in spirits, but I don't believe in fortune-telling.
Though it's different if the fortunes are told with spirits.{p}

What with the Kokkuri-san letter boards written all over it and a suicide having happened there, hardly anyone used that restroom.
Well, you see, a friend of mine fell victim to it.
He was a guy who used that restroom.{p}

That friend's name was Keiichi Tsuda.
It's not like Tsuda used that restroom on purpose or anything.
He knew the stories about it, after all.{p}

The letters on the board stood out more clearly than he'd expected.
He tried his best not to look at it, but it bothered him all the same.
Anyway, he did his business quickly and went outside.{p}

When he turned around and looked back at the restroom, he thought he saw something like a mist swirling inside.
For an instant, a shiver ran down his spine.
Nothing in particular had happened this time, but he thought maybe he'd better never go near that place again.{p}

Tsuda crouched down and seemed to be staring intently at the restroom's letter board.
Then he started licking the letter board, lick, lick.
After licking it for a while, he suddenly stood up and started hopping up and down.{p}

So you wouldn't follow him.
Actually, I didn't follow him either.
I had a bad feeling about it, you see.{p}

"Let's never, ever go there again."
{f:I|I} nodded at Hosoda's words.
And secretly, I thought I might be able to write a good article out of this.
Now, just one person left{f:.|.}
I had to hurry back to the club room and hear the next story{f:...|...}{p}

In the dim evening, his whispering voice drifted softly out of the restroom.
He had lit a candle in there.
Then, once he'd finished peeling off every last scale, he dripped candle wax onto the barely breathing koi.{p}

"You don't wanna make me mad, see.
Ahahahaha."
He laughed.{p}

The next morning, he went to school as usual.
And, maybe because it bothered him a little, he went to check on that restroom.
It would be trouble if any of the koi's remains were left behind, after all.{p}

Before long he got sick of that, so he scraped all the scales off at once with a knife.
The scales came away clean, with a crackling sound.
Then he plunged the knife into the koi's belly, now scraped clean of scales.{p}

And he was disappointed.
The koi had died so quickly...
"And here I was just getting started☆"{p}

So he decided to hide it in the restroom next to the gym.
The teachers hardly ever used that one.
There was almost no chance of it being found.{p}

"Tch. So I killed a tanuki or two. So what?
You got no business lecturing me about it."
With that, the guy walked out.{p}

Then one day, he went to visit Ponkichi's grave for the first time in a while.
And he heard a voice calling him from inside the restroom.
Wondering what it was, he went into the restroom.{p}

"Waaaaah!"
He ran all over the restroom trying to get away.
He wanted to get out of the restroom, but the ghost cut him off at the doorway, so he couldn't escape.{p}

But Ponkichi didn't leave it at that.
Once the ghost was gone, Ponkichi went for him next.
Or maybe 'possessed him' is the right way to put it?{p}

"What, hey, done already?
Oh well, my arms are getting tired anyway..."
When he lifted Ponkichi up, it threw up all the water it had gulped down.{p}

"Whoa! Gross!
If you're gonna puke, puke in the toilet."
With that, he took Ponkichi over to a toilet.{p}

"Whoa, the damn thing's clogged!"
Ponkichi had been flushed down and got its head jammed into the drain where the water goes, so the water couldn't flow and started backing up.
Seeing that, the guy left Ponkichi there and walked out.{p}

A while later, he woke up because something felt cold.
Water was flooding out from the stalls.
He went to look, and...{p}

That the crying was coming from right beside his ear.
In a panic, he leapt away from the spot.
But there was nothing like that where he'd been lying.{p}

Once he saw that face, you know.
Even the guy couldn't stand it anymore.
The whole restroom was full of the smell of burning, too.{p}

"Tch. So I killed a tanuki or two. So what?
You've got no right to lecture me about anything."
With that, the guy walked out.{p}

Sure, got it, I'll tell you.
Nobody would use that restroom anymore.
Not even the teachers.{p}

A scream came from the direction of the restroom.
It was Tsuda's voice.
Tsuda had told me to come help him if anything happened, so I rushed over to the restroom.{p}

Hahahaha, why are you so startled?
You're in exactly the same pose I was in back then.
I crouched down just like that too, with my hands over my head.{p}

..................
The silence went on for a while.
I timidly raised my head.{p}

He was conscious, but he'd lost all sense of self.
Whatever happened, he didn't react.
I wonder how he ended up that way.{p}

Oops, I dragged that out, didn't I?
Sorry, sorry.
Well then, next person, go ahead...{p}

The guy gave Ponkichi a poke, but Ponkichi didn't make a peep.
"What, you went and died on me?"
He said it like he was really disappointed, then left it hanging there and walked out of the restroom.{p}

"Tch. So I killed a tanuki or two. So what?
You've got no right to lecture me about anything."
With that, the guy walked out.{p}

So, about that restroom by the gym.
Apparently it's haunted.
By the student who killed Ponkichi...{p}

Yoshida was still missing.
The boy who'd looked after the tanuki kept tending Ponkichi's grave every day.
Why do you suppose he cared so much about Ponkichi?{p}

I was worried about it, so I followed it up into the hill out back.
There were several tanuki cubs there.
They were waiting for their mother to come home.{p}

I see, that's too bad.
It was just getting to the good part.
But I hate forcing people who don't want to.{p}

"I hated you.
I hated you so, so much...
And on top of that—who knows what you were thinking—you even gave me a name, when I'd become a tanuki.{p}

More importantly, you killed the cubs.
Because you locked me up, those little ones couldn't eat.
It's your fault.{p}
